// panic
// #2615: extends the #1843 stack guard to the one task whose stack is LARGER
// than the 262144-byte identity granule -- `main` itself, at 8 MiB
// (`schedMainStackBytes`, runtime/sched/sched.bit). ./run_spawn_stack_overflow_panic.bit
// proves the guard on a spawned (masked-identity) task; this proves it on the
// other, structurally different path.
//
// WHY THIS IS A DIFFERENT PATH, NOT A BIGGER VERSION OF THE SAME CASE.
// `schedCurrentTask` finds a MASKED task by aligning `sp` down to the
// 262144-byte granule and reading a tag at that base -- which caps a
// masked-identity stack at exactly the granule (sched.bit's own "THIS IS THE
// IDENTITY GRANULE" comment). `main` cannot use that: 8 MiB does not fit one
// granule, so `main`'s base is instead RECORDED at boot, via
// `schedRegisterBigStack` (runtime/root/{darwin,linux}/boot.bit step 5), and
// `schedCurrentTask` only consults that registry as a fallback when masking
// finds no tag. So a spawned task's overflow and main's overflow are caught
// by different mechanisms and, on this runtime, diagnosed differently too:
// `runtime/root/darwin/signal.bit`'s `segvHandler` rounds the fault address
// up to a stack-sized boundary and compares it against `mainStackBaseAddr`
// (an EXACT registry match, not a heuristic) before falling back to the
// granule-alignment guess the spawned-task sibling case exercises. An exact
// match calls `emitMainStackOverflow()`; the heuristic path (or no match at
// all) would print a different message or none.
//
// This case recurses with NO depth bound at all -- not a large constant --
// specifically so it clears main's 8 MiB (32x a spawned task's 256 KiB) and
// runs into the unmapped guard hole `mapGuardedStack` leaves directly below
// `mainStackBaseAddr`. Confirmed by direct execution before this fixture was
// written (`bit build` + run the standalone binary): exit 2, stderr exactly
// "bit: stack overflow\n" (the main-specific message, distinct from
// ./run_spawn_stack_overflow_panic.expected's "...in a spawned task"), and
// NOTHING on stdout -- main's own `print` never runs because the recursion
// never returns. `runtime/root/linux/signal.bit` carries the identical
// `emitMainStackOverflow` message and the identical `mainStackBaseAddr`
// exact-match check, so the diagnostic is not a Darwin-only artifact, though
// only the Darwin path is what this golden run actually exercises.
//
// `1 + down(n + 1)` rather than a bare tail call, matching
// ./run_spawn_stack_overflow_panic.bit's shape: the addition after the
// recursive call is deliberately not tail position, so the compiler cannot
// legally fold this into a loop that would never overflow anything.
fn down(n: int): int {
  return 1 + down(n + 1)
}

fn main() {
  print("unreachable: ${down(0)}\n")
}
